// Unit 5 Final Assessment — 40-question pool

// Lesson 1 — 6
::U5FINAL L1Q1::A hazard is {=a source of possible harm ~proof that injury occurred ~a diagnosis ~a treatment}

::U5FINAL L1Q2::Which action best reduces a wet-floor hazard? {=Mark and clean the area before someone slips ~Ignore it ~Run across it ~Wait for an injury}

::U5FINAL L1Q3::Severe breathing difficulty requires {=immediate emergency assistance ~a routine appointment only ~a quiz first ~peer diagnosis}

::U5FINAL L1Q4::Before helping at an injury scene, first {=check scene safety ~enter any danger immediately ~move every injured person ~post online}

::U5FINAL L1Q5::A prompt but non-life-threatening concern generally requires {=timely professional evaluation ~automatic emergency transport in every case ~no care ~self-diagnosis}

::U5FINAL L1Q6::Which response uses evidence correctly? {=The exposed wire and water raise immediate electrical danger, so keep away and seek qualified help ~One clue proves every outcome ~Ignore the environment ~Touch the wire to test it}

// Lesson 2 — 7
::U5FINAL L2Q1::A communicable disease is one that {=can spread through infectious transmission ~is always chronic ~is caused only by behavior ~cannot be prevented}

::U5FINAL L2Q2::A pathogen is {=a disease-causing organism or agent ~a protective behavior ~a screening result ~an access barrier}

::U5FINAL L2Q3::Which action interrupts respiratory transmission? {=Appropriate ventilation and staying away from others when ill ~Sharing drinks ~Ignoring symptoms ~Touching the face after exposure}

::U5FINAL L2Q4::Exposure means {=contact with a possible source, not proof of infection ~confirmed diagnosis ~guaranteed symptoms ~completed treatment}

::U5FINAL L2Q5::Symptoms alone {=may suggest a need for evaluation but do not always establish diagnosis ~always identify the exact pathogen ~prove who caused infection ~replace testing}

::U5FINAL L2Q6::Which is a community prevention strategy? {=A vaccination or public-health education program ~Sharing misinformation ~Discouraging testing ~Hiding outbreak information}

::U5FINAL L2Q7::After a fictional possible exposure, the strongest conclusion is {=follow credible guidance and seek testing or care when appropriate ~assume infection immediately ~blame the exposed person ~self-prescribe treatment}

// Lesson 3 — 7
::U5FINAL L3Q1::A chronic disease generally {=persists over time and may require ongoing management ~always spreads person to person ~lasts one hour ~is always preventable}

::U5FINAL L3Q2::Which is nonmodifiable? {=Inherited family-history factor ~Tobacco use ~Physical inactivity ~A chosen routine}

::U5FINAL L3Q3::Which is modifiable? {=Tobacco exposure behavior ~Age ~Inherited gene ~Biological family history}

::U5FINAL L3Q4::Family history {=may influence risk without guaranteeing disease ~is a diagnosis ~makes prevention irrelevant ~proves a person's behavior}

::U5FINAL L3Q5::Primary prevention aims to {=reduce risk before disease develops ~confirm diagnosis ~manage complications after diagnosis only ~replace healthcare}

::U5FINAL L3Q6::An abnormal screening result means {=further professional evaluation may be needed ~the disease is definitely diagnosed ~the person is to blame ~treatment must begin without evaluation}

::U5FINAL L3Q7::Strong multifactorial analysis {=considers heredity, environment, behavior, protection, and access while limiting conclusions ~uses one factor as proof ~blames lifestyle ~ignores protective factors}

// Lesson 4 — 7
::U5FINAL L4Q1::An STI may be {=asymptomatic ~always visible ~always bacterial ~a moral judgment}

::U5FINAL L4Q2::Maintained abstinence from intercourse {=prevents pregnancy resulting from intercourse ~guarantees prevention through every nonsexual pathway ~makes consent unnecessary ~is determined by peers}

::U5FINAL L4Q3::Which is accurate? {=Some pregnancy-prevention methods do not protect against STIs ~Every contraceptive prevents STIs ~No symptoms prove no STI ~Appearance confirms testing}

::U5FINAL L4Q4::Risk reduction means {=lowering risk without necessarily eliminating it ~guaranteed zero risk ~self-diagnosis ~ignoring evidence}

::U5FINAL L4Q5::Consent is {=voluntary agreement free from coercion ~silence under pressure ~permanent once given ~obtained through threats}

::U5FINAL L4Q6::A student maintaining an abstinence boundary under pressure should {=state the boundary, disengage if needed, and seek support ~accept peer norms ~provide private details ~treat pressure as consent}

::U5FINAL L4Q7::A fictional possible STI exposure is best addressed through {=appropriate professional testing or healthcare ~appearance ~an anonymous symptom quiz ~stigma}

// Lesson 5 — 7
::U5FINAL L5Q1::Exploitation is {=unfairly taking advantage of another person for someone else's benefit ~respecting a boundary ~routine disagreement ~professional care}

::U5FINAL L5Q2::Fraud involves {=deception ~emergency treatment ~transparent terms ~consent}

::U5FINAL L5Q3::Coercion may involve {=threats, intimidation, pressure, or manipulation ~voluntary agreement ~verified credentials ~routine screening}

::U5FINAL L5Q4::A warning sign {=raises concern but is not automatic proof ~always proves trafficking ~requires confrontation ~should be ignored}

::U5FINAL L5Q5::An opportunity demands secrecy, location data, and travel alone. The safest response is {=do not proceed; disengage and involve trusted support ~continue to investigate ~meet privately ~send identification}

::U5FINAL L5Q6::A friend discloses threats and control. A safe response is {=listen without blame and connect to trusted or emergency help as appropriate ~promise secrecy in immediate danger ~confront the person ~ask why the friend stayed}

::U5FINAL L5Q7::Students should not investigate suspected trafficking because {=continued contact or confrontation can increase danger ~they must first gather legal proof ~trusted resources are unnecessary ~meeting is safer}

// Lesson 6 — 6
::U5FINAL L6Q1::Preventive care includes {=routine services intended to reduce risk or detect concerns early ~emergency treatment only ~anonymous advertising ~self-diagnosis}

::U5FINAL L6Q2::A routine checkup is generally matched with {=appropriate primary or preventive care ~the emergency department in every case ~no care ~an uncredentialed seller}

::U5FINAL L6Q3::A prompt non-emergency concern needs {=timely qualified evaluation ~automatic delay ~emergency care in every case ~social-media diagnosis}

::U5FINAL L6Q4::Severe breathing difficulty requires {=immediate emergency response ~routine navigation first ~cost comparison before help ~a future screening}

::U5FINAL L6Q5::Which service is most credible? {=One with verified credentials, evidence, transparent scope, and realistic claims ~One promising a secret guaranteed cure ~One hiding risks ~One demanding immediate payment without information}

::U5FINAL L6Q6::A transportation barrier may affect access. The strongest response is to {=consider realistic qualified alternatives without blaming the person ~assume the person does not care ~use an uncredentialed service ~delay an emergency}
